RUST CHAIN — telegraphed single-contact formation kit

23 image-generated drawn cels plus one fully transparent terminal cel.
24 separate 512 × 512 straight-RGBA PNGs; 6 × 4 atlas, 3072 × 2048.
Source image, original generation prompt(s), and provenance are included unchanged.

ENEMY: formation/windup 840 ms (cels 1–14 at 60 ms), strike 40 ms,
contact 90 ms, recovery 350 ms (cels 17–23 at 50 ms), clear hold 350 ms.
Total one-shot duration: 1670 ms. One contact at 880 ms (source index 15 / cel 16).

HERO: formation 120 ms (cels 1, 6, 10, 14 at 30 ms), then the same
40 ms strike, 90 ms contact, 350 ms recovery, and 350 ms clear hold.
Total one-shot duration: 950 ms. One contact at 160 ms (source index 15 / cel 16).

The enemy and hero use exactly the same drawn cels. Only timing and formation
cel selection differ. APNGs play once and end clear. MP4s repeat 3 isolated
native-speed cycles at 200 fps, preserving all durations on a 5 ms basis.
MP4 labels show phase, source cel, and the active cel's time interval.

Fixed virtual grid; shared source scale 1.5; shared padding [64.0, 64.0].
Per-row/individual extraction boundaries only exclude neighboring cels.
Crop offset compensation preserves nominal registration. No per-frame bbox
recentering, procedural VFX, generated in-betweens, morphs, or opacity fades.
Only the terminal cel is deliberately replaced with fully transparent pixels.

See timing_manifest.json for both timelines, exact crops, atlas mapping,
pivot, target, footprint, and the single damage-event suggestion.
These coordinates are art-direction guides, not engine collision code.
See qa.json and action_contact.jpg before importing into an engine.

To reproduce from inside this kit directory, with Pillow, NumPy and ffmpeg:
python assembly_script.py --config assembly_config.json --root .. --attacks rust_chain
